"EmbeddingGemma 2 model card  |  Google AI for Developers (https://ai.google.dev/gemma/docs/embeddinggemma/model_card_2)\nciteturn41356view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn41355view0\",\"id\":60}); Total lines: 368\nL0: [Button: ]\nL1: \nL2: cite0†Models†deepmind.google [Button: Solutions ] [Button: Code assistance ] [Button: Community ]\nL3: \nL4: [Input: Search]\nL5: \nL6: `/`\nL7: \nL8:   * English\nL9:   * Deutsch\nL10:   * Español – América Latina\nL11:   * Français\nL12:   * Indonesia\nL13:   * Italiano\nL14:   * Polski\nL15:   * Português – Brasil\nL16:   * Shqip\nL17:   * Tiếng Việt\nL18:   * Türkçe\nL19:   * Русский\nL20:   * עברית\nL21:   * العربيّة\nL22:   * فارسی\nL23:   * हिंदी\nL24:   * বাংলা\nL25:   * ภาษาไทย\nL26:   * 中文 – 简体\nL27:   * 中文 – 繁體\nL28:   * 日本語\nL29:   * 한국어\nL30: Sign in\nL31: \nL32: cite0†Gemma†deepmind.google cite1†Docs L33: \nL34: [Input: Filter]\nL35: \nL36: [Button: ]\nL37: \nL38:   * Models\nL39:   * Core Gemma\nL40: \nL41:     * cite2†Gemma 4 model card L42: \nL43:   * Core Variants\nL44: \nL45:     * Gemma 3n\nL46: \nL47:     * DiffusionGemma\nL48: \nL49:     * FunctionGemma\nL50: \nL51:     * EmbeddingGemma\nL52: \nL53:     * PaliGemma\nL54: \nL55:     * ShieldGemma\nL56: \nL57:   * Run Gemma\nL58: \nL59:   * Fundamentals\nL60: \nL61:     * cite3†Prompt Formatting L62: \nL63:   * Run locally with a Chat UI or integrate via API\nL64: \nL65:   * Run efficiently on Edge\nL66: \nL67:   * Build/Train in Python\nL68: \nL69:     * cite4†Tunix (Tune-in-JAX)†tunix.readthedocs.io L70:     * cite5†Unsloth†unsloth.ai L71: \nL72:   * Deploy to Production / Enterprise\nL73: \nL74:   * Multi-Token Prediction (MTP)\nL75:   * Core Capabilities\nL76: \nL77:   * Text\nL78: \nL79:   * Visual data\nL80: \nL81:   * cite6†Thinking L82:   * Tuning guides\nL83: \nL84:   * cite7†Tune using Gemma library†gemma-llm.readthedocs.io L85:   * Research and tools\nL86: \nL87:   * RecurrentGemma\nL88: \nL89:   * Community\nL90: \nL91:   * Legal\nL92: \nL93: [Button: Send feedback ]\nL94: # EmbeddingGemma 2 model card\nL95: \nL96: cite8†Image L97: \nL98: cite9†Hugging Face†huggingface.co | cite10†GitHub†github.com | cite11†Launch Blog†blog.google | cite12†Documentation |\nL99: License: cite13†Apache 2.0 | Authors: cite14†Google DeepMind†deepmind.google L100: EmbeddingGemma 2 is an open multimodal embedding model built by Google DeepMind which maps text (incl. code), images, video, and audio inputs—and combinations thereof—into a single, unified 768-dimensional vector space. The model has 740M total parameters, combining a 270M parameter text model with modular vision (170M) and audio (300M) encoders.\nL101: Designed to run on consumer hardware such as mobile devices and laptops, EmbeddingGemma 2 delivers low-latency semantic representations for on-device applications, like search, retrieval-augmented generation (RAG), classification, and clustering.\nL102: \nL103: EmbeddingGemma 2 builds upon the architectural and capability advancements of Gemma 4, offering several core features:\nL104:   * Native multimodality: Native multimodality: Unifies 4 modalities (text, images, video, and audio) in a single shared 768-dimensional embedding space.\nL105:   * Multilinguality and code: EmbeddingGemma 2 understands 100+ languages, and achieves a ~14% improvement on code tasks relative to its predecessor.\nL106:   * Flexible footprint: Combines a 270M parameter text backbone (130M transformer + 140M embedder) with selectively loadable vision (170M) and audio (300M) encoders, allowing developers to load only the modalities required for their use case.\nL107:   * Matryoshka Representation Learning (MRL): Native support for truncated embeddings across 128d, 256d, 512d, and 768d, enabling up to a 6x reduction in vector storage costs with minimal impact on quality.\nL108:   * Context length: 8K token context window, capable of processing minutes of audio or video.\nL109:   * Task-steered representations: Uses lightweight text instruction prefixes to optimize embeddings for different tasks (search, classification, clustering, semantic similarity, etc.).\nL110: ### Model Overview\nL111: Parameters  | Total  | 740M\nL112: --- | --- | ---\nL113:  | Backbone  | 130M\nL114:  | Embedder  | 140M\nL115:  | Modality Encoders  | Vision: 170M Audio: 300M\nL116: Architecture  | Layers  | 24\nL117:  | Model Dimension  | 512\nL118:  | Hidden Dimension  | 2048\nL119:  | Sliding Window  | 1024 tokens\nL120:  | Vocabulary Size  | 262,144\nL121:  | # Heads  | 4\nL122:  | # KV-Heads (Local/Global)  | 2/1\nL123:  | Local:Global  | 5:1\nL124:  | Attention  | GQA/MQA\nL125:  | Activation  | Gated FFN with GELU\nL126:  | Pooling  | Mean Pooling\nL127:  | Projection Layer  | 512→768\nL128: Input/Output  | Supported Modalities  | Text, Images, Video, Audio\nL129:  | Context Window  | 8,192 tokens\nL130:  | Native Output Dimension  | 768\nL131:  | MRL Truncation Dimensions  | 128, 256, 512\nL132: ## Benchmark Results\nL133: \nL134: EmbeddingGemma 2 was evaluated across text, code, vision, visual document, video, and audio embedding benchmarks. All results reported below use the full-precision checkpoint.\nL135: ### Overall Evaluation Results (768d)\nL136: Modality  | Benchmark  | Metric  | EmbeddingGemma 2  | EmbeddingGemma 1\nL137: --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL138: Text  | Massive Text Embedding Benchmark (MTEB, multilingual, v2)  | Mean(Task), Multiple  | 61.36  | 61.15\nL139:  | Massive Text Embedding Benchmark (MTEB, code, v1)  | Mean(Task), NDCG@10  | 78.68  | 68.76\nL140: Image  | Massive Image Embedding Benchmark (MIEB, lite)  | Mean(TaskType), Multiple  | 64.64  | -\nL141:  | Massive Multimodal Embedding Benchmark (MMEB v2 - Image)  | Mean(Task), Hit@1  | 57.28  | -\nL142:  | Massive Multimodal Embedding Benchmark (MMEB v2 - VisDoc)  | Mean(Task), NDCG@5  | 67.84  | -\nL143: Video  | Massive Multimodal Embedding Benchmark (MMEB v2 - Video)  | Mean(Task), Hit@1  | 50.67  | -\nL144: Audio  | Massive Sound Embedding Benchmark (MSEB, Retrieval)  | Mean(Task), MRR@10  | 69.54  | -\nL145:  | Massive Audio Embedding Benchmark (MAEB) Hugging Face   | Mean(Task), Multiple  | 49.39  | -\nL146: ### Evaluation Results with Vector Truncation\nL147: \nL148: With MRL, EmbeddingGemma 2 representations can be truncated below the native 768d to 128d, 256d, and 512d representations and re-normalized. With this, model users can reduce storage requirements, with minimal quality impact down to 256d. 128d is best suited to text-only workloads.\nL149: Output Dimension  | Compression Ratio  | MTEB (multilingual, v2) Mean(Task)  | MTEB (eng, v2) Mean(Task)  | MTEB (code, v1) Mean(Task)  | MIEB (lite) Mean(TaskType)  | MMEB (v2) Overall  | MSEB (Retrieval) Mean(Task)  | MAEB Mean(Task)\nL150: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL151: 768d (Full)  | 1:1  | 61.36  | 68.46  | 78.68  | 64.64  | 59.01  | 69.54  | 49.39\nL152: 512d  | 1:1.5  | 61.17  | 68.41  | 77.24  | 64.32  | 58.38  | 69.18  | 49.21\nL153: 256d  | 1:3  | 60.41  | 67.78  | 76.18  | 63.13  | 56.24  | 66.76  | 48.91\nL154: 128d  | 1:6  | 57.89  | 65.68  | 71.41  | 59.06  | 45.65  | 56.71  | 46.92\nL155: ## Quick Start\nL156: \nL157: Install the `sentence-transformers` library:\nL158: \nL159:     `pip install -U sentence-transformers transformers\nL160:     `\nL161: \nL162: Generate text embeddings: ```python from sentence_transformers import SentenceTransformer\nL163: \nL164: model = SentenceTransformer(\"google/embeddinggemma-2\")\nL165: \nL166: query = \"What causes the northern lights?\" document = \"The northern lights are caused by charged particles from the sun.\"\nL167: query_emb = model.encode(query, prompt_name=\"SearchQuery\") doc_emb = model.encode(document, prompt_name=\"Document\") print(model.similarity(query_emb, doc_emb)) ```\nL168: ## Best Practices\nL169: \nL170: For optimal embedding quality and runtime efficiency, follow these configurations and best practices:\nL171: ### 1. Task Instruction Prefixes\nL172: \nL173: EmbeddingGemma 2 is trained with short task instruction prefixes prepended to text inputs. Using the right prefix improves quality; omitting it still works but reduces precision. Prefixes apply to text only. Pass images, video, and audio without any prefix.\nL174: \nL175: Documents with a real title should be formatted as `title: {title} | text: {content}`. Use `title: none` when no title is available.\nL176: \nL177: Prefix Notation & Usage\nL178: We offer two types of task prefixes, depending on how embeddings are used in the task. There are two types of tasks:\nL179:   * Asymmetric Tasks (e.g. retrieval): Use a query prefix for queries and a document prefix for corpus items.\nL180:   * Symmetric Tasks (e.g. classification, similarity): Apply the same task prefix to all inputs being compared.\nL181: Use Case  | Task Type  | Prompt Name  | Query Task Instruction  | Document Task Instruction (use `none` if no title)\nL182: --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL183: Web / document search  | Asymmetric  | SearchQuery  | `task: search result | query: {query}`  | `title: {title} | text: {content}`\nL184: Question answering  | Asymmetric  | QuestionAnswering  | `task: question answering | query: {question}`  | `title: {title} | text: {passage}`\nL185: Fact checking  | Asymmetric  | FactChecking  | `task: fact checking | query: {claim}`  | `title: {title} | text: {evidence}`\nL186: Code search  | Asymmetric  | CodeRetrieval  | `task: code retrieval | query: {query}`  | `title: {title or filename} | text: {code}`\nL187: Text classification  | Symmetric  | Classification  | `task: classification | query: {content}`  | N/A\nL188: Clustering  | Symmetric  | Clustering  | `task: clustering | query: {content}`  | N/A\nL189: Measuring similarity  | Symmetric  | SentenceSimilarity  | `task: sentence similarity | query: {content}`  | N/A\nL190: Please note: `prompt_name=\"Document\"` applies `title: none`; titled documents must still be formatted manually, like `model.encode(f\"title: {title} | text: {document}\")`\nL191: ### 2. Selective Encoder Loading\nL192: \nL193: The vision and audio encoders are independent components. To reduce memory consumption when deploying text-only or single-modality pipelines, disable unused modality encoders via SentenceTransformer's `config_kwargs`:\nL194: \nL195: Please note: configuring EmbeddingGemma 2 to load with omission of some encoders differs among model libraries; please refer to the appropriate documentation for more information on this.\nL196: Active Modalities  | `config_kwargs`  | Effective Size\nL197: --- | --- | ---\nL198: Text only  | `{\"vision_config\": None, \"audio_config\": None}`  | 270M\nL199: Text and image  | `{\"audio_config\": None}`  | 440M\nL200: Text and audio  | `{\"vision_config\": None}`  | 570M\nL201: Full multimodal  | `{}`  | 740M\nL202: ### 3. Matryoshka Dimension Truncation\nL203: \nL204: EmbeddingGemma 2 is trained with Matryoshka Representation Learning, so the 768-dimensional output vector can be shortened by keeping only its leading dimensions. The supported dimensions are 768, 512, 256, and 128. Shorter vectors reduce storage and speed up similarity search at some cost to quality.\nL205: \nL206: At runtime, please adhere to the following guidelines:\nL207:   * Re-normalize after truncating: slicing a unit-length vector does not preserve unit length. The shortened vector must be L2-normalized before it is used for cosine similarity. Skipping this step degrades ranking quality silently—it produces plausible-looking scores rather than an error.\nL208:   * Queries and documents must share a dimension. A 768-dimensional query cannot be scored against a 128-dimensional corpus.\nL209:   * To avoid truncating and normalizing yourself, you should pass in the `truncate_dim` and `normalize_embeddings` fields when calling `model.encode()`:\nL210: \nL211:     `query_emb = model.encode(\nL212:        query,\nL213:        truncate_dim=128, # or 512, 256\nL214:        normalize_embeddings=True,\nL215:     )\nL216:     `\nL217: Model quality at each dimension is reported in the truncation table in the Benchmark Results section above. Quality is close to lossless down to 256 dimensions. 128 dimensions degrades multimodal quality substantially and should be validated against your own workload before adoption.\nL218: ### 4. Numerical Precision\nL219: \nL220: Run inference in `bfloat16` or `float32`. Do not use `float16`.\nL221: \nL222: EmbeddingGemma 2's activation range exceeds the dynamic range of `float16`. In `float16` the model returns NaN or silently degraded embeddings rather than raising an error, so the failure is easy to miss.\nL223: `bfloat16` is safe, and carries the same 8-bit exponent as `float32`. This is the recommended default on hardware with native support, and halves memory use relative to `float32`. Use `float32` elsewhere, including on most CPUs.\nL224: In SentenceTransformers, you can set this via `model_kwargs`, and determine it programmatically via `is_bf16_supported`:\nL225: \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBring multimodal semantic search to the edge with EmbeddingGemma 2 - Google Developers Blog (https://developers.googleblog.com/google-ai-edge-with-embeddinggemma-2)\nciteturn41356view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn41355view0\",\"id\":64}); Redirected to URL: https://developers.googleblog.com/google-ai-edge-with-embeddinggemma-2/; Total lines: 147\n"
